            "abstract": "The methodologies of Digital Humanities (DH) are acquiring an ever greater relevance to the study of the history of reading, for example through the collation of historical reading experiences in databases. The UK Reading Experience Database (UK-RED; Eliot, 1996). is one of the longest-running Digital Humanities projects in the United Kingdom, having collected in 20 years about 40,000 curated testimonies of reading dated between 1450 and 1945. As a corpus, UK-RED provides opportunities for both macro- and micro-scale research, combining corpus approaches with close reading and comparison of individual testimonies of reading (Crone and Halsey, 2012). This chapter will investigate how corpus linguistics approaches can be applied to the analysis of Digital Humanities resources such as UK-RED. It will examine the project’s data as a corpus, searching for language patterns in accounts of experiences of reading and examining the reading testimonies across the five-century span of the project. The application of corpus methods allows the interrogation of reading practices on a scale impossible with human means alone. The interpretation and critique of the results of these analyses can in turn stimulate new questions and signal paths for further investigations, such as those of the recent READ-IT project on contemporary digital reading practices",
